From 99db25c6a8ff102b6bdbfe660916c885a9645c20 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Akshat Singh <automobilehacks@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:21:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] econet-eth: don't lose the first RX interrupt

The interrupt handler masks a ring's interrupt and schedules its NAPI,
and only the NAPI poll unmasks it again. If the interrupt comes while
NAPI is disabled, napi_schedule() does nothing and the interrupt stays
masked for good. The ring is then never serviced again: it fills up,
and the GDM drops every frame after that.

en75_qdma_use() turned DMA on before enabling NAPI. Frames that had
queued up in the GDM while DMA was off landed straight away, and on a
busy network their interrupt often came before NAPI was ready. On the
Nokia G-140W-C this left the router deaf on some boots: eth0 counted
the 511 frames that filled the ring (plus what the GDM had buffered)
and nothing after them, the RX interrupt was masked and the driver's
irqmask had RX cleared too, so setting the bit in hardware was undone
by the next interrupt.

Enable NAPI before DMA. Also poll every ring once after enabling, which
catches an interrupt that came while NAPI was being disabled at close:
each poll handles whatever is waiting and unmasks its interrupt when it
completes.

Link: https://github.com/cjdelisle/econet_eth/pull/19
Signed-off-by: Akshat Singh <automobilehacks@gmail.com>
---
 econet_qdma.c | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/econet_qdma.c
+++ b/econet_qdma.c
@@ -1141,16 +1141,39 @@ int en75_qdma_use(struct en75_qdma *qdma)
 	if (qdma->users++ > 0)
 		return 0;
 
+	/* The interrupt handler masks a ring's interrupt and schedules its
+	 * NAPI, and only the NAPI poll unmasks it again. If the interrupt
+	 * comes while NAPI is disabled, scheduling does nothing and the
+	 * interrupt stays masked for good: the ring is never serviced
+	 * again, it fills up and the GDM drops everything after that.
+	 *
+	 * So enable NAPI before DMA. Frames that queued up in the GDM while
+	 * DMA was off land as soon as it is on, and their interrupt must
+	 * find NAPI ready.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(qdma->q_rx); i++)
+		napi_enable(&qdma->q_rx[i].napi);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(qdma->q_tx_done); i++)
+		napi_enable(&qdma->q_tx_done[i].napi);
+
 	qcfg = en75_rreg(&qdma->regs->qdma_cfg);
 	set_qregs_qcfg_rx_dma_en(&qcfg, true);
 	set_qregs_qcfg_tx_dma_en(&qcfg, true);
 	en75_wreg(qcfg, &qdma->regs->qdma_cfg);
 
+	/* An interrupt can also come while NAPI is being disabled, when the
+	 * device is closed, and stay masked. Poll every ring once: that
+	 * handles anything already waiting, and each poll unmasks its
+	 * interrupt when it completes.
+	 */
+	local_bh_disable();
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(qdma->q_rx); i++)
-		napi_enable(&qdma->q_rx[i].napi);
+		napi_schedule(&qdma->q_rx[i].napi);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(qdma->q_tx_done); i++)
-		napi_enable(&qdma->q_tx_done[i].napi);
+		napi_schedule(&qdma->q_tx_done[i].napi);
+	local_bh_enable();
 
 	return 0;
 }
